WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Democratic Education is based on the belief

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.120
that children themselves know best
what is good for them.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.220
The job of a school is to provide a democratic structure

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.320
in which kids can discover their interests

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.560
and learn how to live in a community
that solves conflicts together.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.740
The ideal: to grow up in absence of fear and
to become responsible,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.800
empathic and self-determined citizens.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.280
The idea is not new.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.260
In 1693 the philosopher John Locke wrote that

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.680
"things children learn, should never be
a burden to them."

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.180
Some 200 years later

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.580
the writer Leo Tolstoy opened a school for peasant children on his estate in Russia.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.320
The school evolved from ideas introduced 
by teachers and pupils.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.700
One of the principles was that the students had the right to not listen to the teacher.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.560
The first democratic school that still exists,
is Summerhill in Suffolk, England.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.860
It was founded in 1921 by A.S. Neill

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.760
who believed that a school should be made for the child, rather than the other way around.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.860
Class attendance is voluntary.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.740
Some children may spend weeks in the woods 
or do nothing all day,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.620
while others choose to learn math 
or try out reading class.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.580
When they grow older many turn to traditional subjects with full focus

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.380
when they realise that they need them
for college entrance exams.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.080
At weekly school meetings, 
staff and students gather to solve conflicts.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.120
Once a conflict is brought to the meeting,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.940
everybody in the school can speak up, mediate
or come up with a solution.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.580
After the conflict was discussed and democratically processed,

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.600
harsh feelings are often buried and staff
and students leave peacefully.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.660
The meetings are also used to work on the school regulations

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.160
and anybody can make a proposal for 
a change of the rules.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.280
If one student requests a change,

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.680
members discuss and then decide by popular vote whether the new idea will be implemented.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.100
Sometimes the kids may vote 
for the abolishment of all rules.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.400
But after a few days of total chaos,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:08.060
students usually use the same democratic process to  re-implement order.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.180
Almost as if they all naturally seek structure.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.600
In the 1960s young educators from all over

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.940
took inspiration from Summerhill and opened so called “free schools”.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.280
One was the Sudbury Valley School which was founded in Massachusetts, United States.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.660
Sudbury took the ideals of a democratic education even further.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.280
Once a year the school meeting is used to re-elect teachers and principals.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.060
Those that don’t receive majority vote will be replaced.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.320
Today there are many independent Sudbury and other democratic schools

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.560
all over the world spanning from Brazil to Israel to Thailand.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.980
Using different structures,

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:53.560
they all promote the idea that students and teachers should have an equal say in what to learn

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.540
and how to work with each other.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.900
The goal is to give children a deep sense for equality and justice.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.420
Albert Einstein famously said: “The only thing that interferes with my learning is my education”.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.440
What do you think,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.180
would a democratic education work for you?

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.820
Please share your thoughts in the comments below!

